\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

This study suggests that the variance structure of LLM evaluation is format-associated. Across three multiple-choice benchmarks, item-related variance---item difficulty plus model$\times$item differential item functioning (DIF)---concentrates at 68--72\%, while on free-form benchmarks (GSM8K, MATH) it disperses to 31--41\%, with no overlap across all five benchmarks. Model$\times$item interaction (up to 36\%) is the second-largest source in cross-model analysis, consistent with DIF patterns identified in IRT research: models differ in which items they find difficult, not in overall ability. Our work introduces G-theory as a multi-facet variance decomposition framework for LLM benchmark evaluation, enabling format-associated comparison and actionable design budgets.

The format-conditional structure implies distinct optimization strategies. For multiple-choice benchmarks, item pool composition is the primary reliability lever; varying prompts or seeds contributes negligible variance. For free-form benchmarks, model identity and model$\times$prompt interaction matter as much as item selection, and evaluation protocols should systematically vary both. The multi-facet efficiency gain is itself format-conditional: MC benchmarks show a 10--12$\times$ ratio while FF benchmarks show 21--22$\times$, indicating that single-condition evaluation of reasoning tasks incurs double the reliability penalty. BF16 and FP16 are interchangeable under binary scoring on single-GPU inference, which eliminates precision as a required control variable. The released D-study calculator enables practitioners to determine the number of items, models, and conditions needed for a target reliability before data collection.

Two extensions are planned. Multi-GPU tensor parallelism will test whether precision separability holds under distributed numerical non-associativity. Fine-tuning variance analysis will quantify how adaptation reshapes the item difficulty profiles that drive model$\times$item interaction.

\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

Our findings are bounded by three scope constraints. First, the model sample comprises eight open-weight 7--9B parameter models; the results do not generalize to API-based models, larger models, or fine-tuned variants. Second, all experiments run on single-GPU inference (NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000); multi-GPU tensor parallelism introduces additional numerical non-associativity that our design does not capture. Third, the benchmark sample covers three multiple-choice and two free-form tasks; other evaluation formats such as code generation, long-form text production, and multi-turn dialogue may exhibit different variance structures.

